course: english
lesson: 52
title: SATs: grammar, punctuation and spelling paper
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The GPS paper tests grammar, punctuation and spelling: a 45-minute paper of about 50 short questions, plus a separate spelling test of 20 words.
explain: Many questions ask what job a word does: an adjective describes a noun ("a quick fox"), and an adverb tells how, when or where something happens ("ran quickly", "ran yesterday").
explain: Little words change everything: "Circle the word that is NOT a noun" asks the opposite of "Circle the noun", so read the whole question.
explain: Some questions ask for Standard English, the form used in tests and formal writing: "we were", not the dialect "we was", which is fine at home but loses the mark here.
explain: Picture Ella saying "Fronted adverbials are bussin'" ("bussin'" means great): a fronted adverbial is a phrase at the start of a sentence telling when, where or how, followed by a comma, as in "After lunch, we played."
fact: The grammar and punctuation paper takes 45 minutes and is worth 50 marks; the spelling test has 20 words.
fact: In the spelling test, each word is read aloud on its own and in a sentence, and you write it in a gap.
fact: An adjective describes a noun ("a tall tree"); an adverb describes a verb, telling how, when or where ("she sang loudly").
fact: A fronted adverbial comes at the start of a sentence and is followed by a comma: "Later that day, it snowed."
fact: A semicolon can join two full sentences that are closely linked: "It was raining; we stayed in."
fact: If one side of a semicolon could not stand alone as a sentence, the semicolon is wrong there.
fact: In the passive, the thing the action happens to comes first: "The cat chased the mouse" becomes "The mouse was chased by the cat."
fact: When you rewrite a sentence in the passive, keep the same tense: past stays past.
fact: "We was" is a dialect form; the Standard English form is "we were", and Standard English is what the test asks for.
fact: Missing a small word like "not" can turn a right answer into a wrong one.
fact: Leaving about five minutes to check catches careless mistakes.
fact: Grammar terms are useful because they let you talk about how sentences work, though nobody needs to know every term perfectly.
fact: "Mardy" is a Leeds and Midlands word for grumpy or sulky.
exercise: How long is the grammar and punctuation paper? | 45 minutes | 5 minutes | 2 hours | All morning long
exercise: How many words are in the SATs spelling test? | 20 | 5 | 100 | 50
exercise: How are the spelling test words given? | Read aloud, alone and in a sentence | Shown one by one on a big screen | Written up on the board for copying | Spelled out letter by letter by the teacher
exercise: "The beautiful fox ran quickly." Which word is the adverb? | quickly | beautiful | ran | fox
exercise: Asked for the adverb in "The red bus stopped suddenly", Kai circles "red". Which word should he have circled? | suddenly | bus | stopped | red
exercise: Which sentence starts with a fronted adverbial? | Before dawn, the birds sang. | The birds sang before dawn. | The birds sang loudly at dawn. | The birds, which were small, sang.
exercise: Which punctuation mark follows a fronted adverbial? | A comma | A full stop | A semicolon | A question mark
exercise: In which sentence is the semicolon used correctly? | It was raining; we stayed in. | It was; raining we stayed in. | It; was raining we stayed in. | It was raining we; stayed in.
exercise: Why is "Because it rained; we stayed in." wrong? | "Because it rained" can't stand alone | Semicolons are never allowed in tests | "We stayed in" is too short a sentence | It needs a question mark at the end
exercise: Rewrite "The dog chased the ball" in the passive. | The ball was chased by the dog. | The ball is chased by the dog. | The dog was chasing the ball. | The ball chases after the dog.
exercise: Leo misses the "not" in "Tick the word that is NOT a verb". What is likely to happen? | He ticks a verb, the opposite | Nothing, as "not" is a filler | He scores two marks instead | He ends up ticking nothing
exercise: Kai writes "we was" in the test. What should it be? | we were | we is | we was being | we wus
exercise: A mum in Leeds says, "Don't get mardy, love, it's only a test." What does "mardy" mean? | Grumpy | Clever | Late | Messy
exercise: Why leave five minutes at the end of the paper? | To check and catch mistakes | To have a short nap on the desk | To start on a brand new paper | To chat quietly with a friend
exercise: Ella says fronted adverbials are "bussin'". What does she mean? | They are great | They are boring | They are confusing | They are rude
exercise: Is spelling tested in the SATs? | Yes, in its own test | No, not at all | Only in the reading paper | Only for some children
test: Your mate says, "Grammar words are useless, you just talk, innit." Put him right. | They let you talk about how sentences work | Nobody ever uses grammar words for anything | Grammar words are only for teachers to use | Grammar words just make you sound posher
test: Aisling in Dublin: "Sure we was only messin', so we were." ("Messin'" means joking around.) Which form does the GPS test want? | we were | we was | we is | we be
test: What would happen if you put "The cat chased the mouse" into the passive but changed the tense by mistake? | You might write "is chased" and lose the mark | Nothing, as the tense never matters at all | It would turn into a question, not a sentence | It would turn into a fronted adverbial
test: A cartoon says, "A semicolon's just a fancy comma, stick it anywhere." Fix it. | It joins two linked full sentences | It goes after every third word | It replaces every single full stop | The cartoon is right; anywhere works
test: Which sentence uses an adverb? | The boy laughed loudly. | The loud boy laughed. | The boy was very loud. | The boy's laugh was loud.
test: "Sod it, I'll just guess the lot," says Dan, with 10 minutes left. What would help more? | Reading each question fully and checking | Guessing everything and leaving early | Rubbing out all of his answers | Starting again from the very beginning
test: Which sentence is in the passive? | The window was broken by Sam. | Sam broke the window. | Sam is breaking the window. | Sam will break the window tomorrow.
test: "Circle the word that is NOT an adjective: tall, happy, slowly, orange." Which word? | slowly | tall | happy | orange
test: Which sentence is punctuated correctly? | Later that day, it snowed. | Later that day it, snowed. | Later, that day it snowed | Later that day; it snowed.
test: Rewrite "Mum baked a cake" in the passive. | A cake was baked by Mum. | A cake is baked by Mum. | Mum was baking a cake. | Mum had baked a cake today.
test: "Quickly, the boy, who was late, ran to school." Which word is the adverb, and where is it? | "Quickly", fronted at the start | "boy", fronted at the start | "late", in the middle | "school", at the end
exam: Identify the word class of "quickly" in the sentence "The fox ran quickly." | Adverb | Adjective | Noun | Preposition
exam: It is given that a sentence reads "The dog chased the ball." Select its correct rendering in the passive voice. | The ball was chased by the dog. | The ball is chased by the dog. | The dog has chased the ball. | The dog was chasing the ball.
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes the Standard English form required in the examination. | We were late. | We was late again. | We is late. | We be late.
exam: Determine the punctuation mark that may properly conjoin two closely related independent clauses. | The semicolon | The apostrophe | The hyphen | The question mark
